    <h1>Soil Analysis: An Introduction to Laboratory Methods</h1>
    <div class="meta">Series: Soil Analysis Laboratory • Part 1 • Published: November 2026</div>

    <p>Soil analysis is the systematic chemical, physical, and biological examination of soil samples to determine their composition, fertility, and environmental quality. It supports agriculture, environmental monitoring, construction, and geochemical research. A reliable soil analysis begins with correct sampling and ends with validated instrumental measurement.</p>

    <h2>1. Sampling and Preparation</h2>
    <p>Representative sampling is the foundation of every reliable result. A field is typically divided into uniform zones, and multiple sub-samples are collected from each zone using a stainless-steel auger or corer. These sub-samples are combined into a composite sample, which is then air-dried at &le; 40 &deg;C to preserve labile constituents.</p>
    <p>After drying, the soil is disaggregated and passed through a 2 mm stainless-steel sieve. Material retained on the sieve (gravel, roots, debris) is recorded and discarded. A separate portion is dried at 105 &deg;C to determine the moisture factor, allowing results to be reported on an oven-dry basis.</p>

    <h2>2. Chemical Extraction and Digestion</h2>
    <p>Different analytical questions require different preparation methods:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Plant-available nutrients</strong> are extracted with mild reagents. The Mehlich-3 extraction, for example, uses acetic acid, ammonium nitrate, ammonium fluoride, nitric acid, and EDTA to estimate available phosphorus, potassium, calcium, magnesium, and micronutrients.</li>
      <li><strong>Environmental metals</strong> are assessed by acid digestion. EPA Method 3050B uses concentrated nitric acid and hydrogen peroxide at approximately 95 &deg;C to release environmentally available trace and heavy metals.</li>
      <li><strong>Soil pH and electrical conductivity (EC)</strong> are measured in a 1:2 soil-to-water suspension using calibrated pH and EC meters.</li>
    </ul>

