    <h2>3. Instrumental Analysis</h2>
    <p>Extracts and digests are analyzed by spectroscopic techniques. Inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES) is widely used for multi-element determination because of its sensitivity and wide linear range. Flame atomic absorption spectroscopy (FAAS) remains common for single-element measurements. Ion chromatography and spectrophotometry are used for anions such as nitrate, sulfate, and phosphate. Organic contaminants are typically analyzed by gas chromatography–mass spectrometry (GC-MS) or liquid chromatography–mass spectrometry (LC-MS).</p>

    <h2>4. Quality Control</h2>
    <p>No analytical result is trustworthy without quality control. Laboratories routinely include method blanks, duplicate sub-samples, matrix spikes, and certified reference materials (CRMs). Recovery rates for CRMs should typically fall within 80–120% of certified values. Calibration curves must be verified with independent check standards, and results should be reported with appropriate uncertainty.</p>

    <h2>5. Interpretation and Reporting</h2>
    <p>Soil data must be interpreted in context. Nutrient levels are compared with crop-specific guidelines, while metal concentrations are evaluated against regulatory thresholds such as those set by environmental agencies. Results are reported on a dry-weight basis with clear units (e.g., mg/kg, cmol(+)/kg) and accompanied by method references and detection limits.</p>

    <h2>Conclusion</h2>
    <p>Soil analysis combines careful field sampling, controlled laboratory preparation, and validated instrumental measurement. Each step influences the final result, and strict quality control is essential. Whether the goal is optimizing crop nutrition or assessing environmental risk, a well-designed soil analysis program provides the reliable data on which sound decisions depend.</p>
